// Living Environment Regents — January 2018
export default {
  id: 'li-jan-2018',
  subject: 'living-environment',
  year: 2018,
  session: 'January',
  totalMinutes: 180,
  questions: [
    { number: 1, part: 'A', text: 'Which organisms and set of characteristics are correctly paired?', choices: ['fungi—carry out photosynthesis and heterotrophic nutrition', 'plants—carry out respiration and autotrophic nutrition', 'decomposers—carry out photosynthesis and autotrophic nutrition', 'animals—carry out autotrophic nutrition and heterotrophic nutrition'], topic: 'Cell Biology', correct: 1, explanation: 'Plants perform both cellular respiration (to release energy from food) and autotrophic nutrition (photosynthesis to make their own food using sunlight), making choice B the only correctly paired option.', diveDeep: 'All living organisms carry out cellular respiration; it is not exclusive to animals. Plants are unique in being autotrophs — they produce their own food via photosynthesis AND respire to release that energy. Fungi are heterotrophs (they absorb nutrients from dead/decaying matter), not photosynthesizers. Decomposers are heterotrophs, not autotrophs. Animals are entirely heterotrophic — they cannot make their own food. A common Regents trap is claiming that plants only photosynthesize and do not respire — this is false. Plants respire 24 hours a day and only photosynthesize when light is available.' },
    { number: 2, part: 'A', text: 'Humans have an effect on ecosystems when they use native grasslands or forested areas for farming or urban use. One negative effect of these changes on the ecosystem is that there will be', choices: ['less biodiversity', 'more homes', 'successful economic growth', 'increased food production'], topic: 'Ecology', correct: 0, image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/q2.png', explanation: 'Converting natural habitats to farmland or urban areas destroys the diverse plant and animal communities that live there, directly reducing biodiversity — the variety of species in an ecosystem.', diveDeep: 'Habitat destruction is the leading cause of biodiversity loss worldwide. When forests or grasslands are converted to farms or cities, native species lose their food sources, shelter, and breeding grounds. Reduced biodiversity makes ecosystems less stable and less resilient to environmental stresses. While farming and urban development may bring economic benefits (choices B, C, D), these are not negative ecological effects — the question asks specifically for a negative environmental impact. On the Regents, human impact on ecosystems almost always involves reduced biodiversity, habitat loss, or disrupted nutrient cycles.' },
    { number: 3, part: 'A', text: 'The diagram below represents structures found in the female reproductive system. A A If the areas labeled A were completely blocked on both sides, the most likely result would be that', choices: ['egg and estrogen production would stop', 'sperm and insulin production would stop', 'fertilization would not occur', 'an embryo would develop'], topic: 'Reproduction', correct: 2, image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/q3.png', explanation: 'The areas labeled A represent the fallopian tubes (oviducts). If both are blocked, sperm cannot reach the egg and the egg cannot travel to the uterus, preventing fertilization from occurring.', diveDeep: 'In human reproduction, fertilization normally occurs in the fallopian tubes where sperm meets the egg. The egg is released from the ovary during ovulation and travels through the fallopian tube toward the uterus. If both tubes are blocked, neither sperm can reach the egg nor can the fertilized egg implant — making fertilization impossible. Eggs are produced in the ovaries (not the fallopian tubes), so egg production would not stop (ruling out choice A). Estrogen is produced by the ovaries regardless of tube blockage. This is the biological basis of tubal ligation as a contraceptive procedure.' },
    { number: 4, part: 'A', text: 'Scientists have studied oceanic plastic garbage "patches" around the world. These are areas that accumulate plastic garbage from coastal regions. Their environmental effect ranges from killing sea life to blocking sunlight from reaching photosynthetic organisms. Without a change in human plastic usage, new garbage patches will continue to form. Which human activity would most directly reduce the amount of plastic garbage that enters the ocean?', choices: ['Ban the production and usage of all bags made from recycled plastic.', 'Clean up plastic trash from shorelines, rivers, and other waterways that ﬂow into the oceans.', 'Manufacture fewer reusable water bottles, so that people will be more likely to use disposable ones.', 'Implement a glass bottle deposit system to discourage people from recycling plastic bottles.'], topic: 'Cell Biology', correct: 1, explanation: 'Cleaning up plastic from shorelines, rivers, and waterways directly intercepts plastic before it reaches the ocean, most directly reducing ocean plastic pollution compared to the other choices.', diveDeep: 'The question asks for the most "direct" action to reduce ocean plastic entry. Choice A bans recycled plastic bags but does not address the existing plastic problem or other types of plastic entering waterways. Choice C increases disposable bottle use — the opposite of what would help. Choice D discourages plastic bottle recycling, which would increase plastic waste. Only choice B directly removes existing plastic from the pathways (rivers, shorelines) that lead to the ocean. On the Regents, read carefully for qualifiers like "most directly" — they signal you must choose the action with the most immediate and targeted effect on the stated problem.' },
    { number: 5, part: 'A', text: 'Monarch butterﬂies migrate from the U.S. and Canada to Mexico every winter. Over the past 10 years, there has been a drastic decrease in the number of monarch butterﬂies. Scientists have estimated that the population may have decreased from about + billion to 35 million. Which action would not be considered a reason for the decline in monarch butterﬂy populations?', choices: ['illegal deforestation', 'extreme temperature changes', 'decreasing food supplies', 'habitat preservation'], topic: 'General', correct: 3, image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/q5.png', explanation: 'Habitat preservation would protect and maintain the monarch butterfly\'s environment, which would help the population recover — it is the opposite of a reason for decline. All other choices are genuine threats to the monarchs.', diveDeep: 'This is a "NOT" question — it asks which option is NOT a cause of decline. Illegal deforestation destroys overwintering forest habitats in Mexico; extreme temperature changes affect migration timing and milkweed availability; decreasing food supplies (mainly milkweed loss) directly reduce caterpillar survival. Habitat preservation, by contrast, is a conservation strategy that would support population recovery. On the Regents, always pay close attention to negating words like "not," "except," or "least likely." Overlooking these words is one of the most common errors on multiple-choice exams. Circle or underline the negative word before analyzing choices.' },
    { number: 6, part: 'A', text: 'Finches on the Galapagos Islands express a variety of traits. Variability in the offspring of these ﬁnches is a result of', choices: ['mutation and cloning', 'meiosis and mutation', 'mitosis and asexual reproduction', 'mitosis and genetic recombination Part A Answer all questions in this part. Directions (1–30): For each statement or question, record on the separate answer sheet the number of the word or expression that, of those given, best completes the statement or answers the question. Living Environment–Jan. \'18'], topic: 'Genetics', correct: 1, explanation: 'Genetic variability in sexually reproducing organisms like finches results from meiosis (which produces genetically unique gametes through crossing over and independent assortment) and mutations (random changes in DNA sequence).', diveDeep: 'Meiosis introduces genetic variation through two mechanisms: crossing over (exchange of genetic material between homologous chromosomes in prophase I) and independent assortment (random alignment of chromosome pairs). Mutations add additional variation by altering DNA sequences. Cloning and asexual reproduction produce genetically identical offspring (no variation). Mitosis alone does not create variation. This genetic diversity in Galapagos finches is what allowed natural selection to produce different beak shapes adapted to different food sources — the key example Darwin used to develop his theory of evolution. Variation + selection = evolution.' },
    { number: 7, part: 'A', text: 'Exposure to certain environmental toxins, such as pesticides, may reduce fertility in males by interfering with their ability to produce gametes. These toxins are most likely having an effect on the', choices: ['testes and progesterone', 'ovaries and testosterone', 'ovaries and estrogen', 'testes and testosterone'], topic: 'Reproduction', correct: 3, image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/q7.png', explanation: 'In males, sperm (gametes) are produced in the testes, and testosterone is the primary male sex hormone that regulates sperm production. Toxins reducing male fertility would therefore affect the testes and testosterone.', diveDeep: 'The question specifies male fertility and male gamete production. Sperm are produced in the seminiferous tubules of the testes through a process regulated by testosterone (and FSH/LH from the pituitary). The ovaries are female reproductive organs (not relevant for males). Progesterone and estrogen are primarily female sex hormones. Understanding the difference between male and female reproductive hormones and organs is a common Regents topic. Testosterone plays roles in both sperm production and secondary male sexual characteristics. Environmental endocrine disruptors (like certain pesticides) can mimic or block hormones, disrupting reproduction.' },
    { number: 8, part: 'A', text: 'Which statement best describes an important process carried out by structure X? x', choices: ['Milk passes from the mother to the fetus.', 'Materials are exchanged between fetal and maternal blood.', 'Maternal blood is converted into fetal blood.', 'Oxygen diffuses from fetal blood to maternal blood.'], topic: 'General', correct: 1, image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/q8.png', explanation: 'Structure X represents the placenta, whose primary function is to facilitate the exchange of materials (oxygen, nutrients, and waste products) between the maternal and fetal bloodstreams, which do not directly mix.', diveDeep: 'The placenta is a temporary organ that forms during pregnancy. Maternal and fetal blood do not mix — substances are exchanged across the thin placental membrane by diffusion and active transport. Oxygen and nutrients move from mother to fetus; carbon dioxide and waste move from fetus to mother. Choice C (blood conversion) is biologically impossible. Choice D reverses the direction of oxygen diffusion — oxygen moves from maternal blood (high O₂) to fetal blood (low O₂), not the other way. Milk is produced in mammary glands after birth, not via the placenta. The placenta is one of the most frequently tested structures in Living Environment fetal development questions.' },
    { number: 9, part: 'A', text: 'Traditional lightbulbs are only 10% efﬁcient. Ninety percent of the energy they use is converted to heat. Modern lightbulbs are much more efﬁcient, but may cost three times as much as traditional lightbulbs. Consumers who switch to modern lightbulbs are most likely', choices: ['spending more money for no good reason', 'trying to stop pollution of the oceans', 'trading a short-term cost for long-term savings', 'helping traditional lightbulb factories employ people'], topic: 'Ecology', correct: 2, explanation: 'Modern efficient lightbulbs cost more upfront but use significantly less electricity over time, resulting in lower energy bills — this is a classic short-term financial investment for long-term savings.', diveDeep: 'This question applies energy efficiency principles in an ecological and economic context. The 10% energy rule (similar to the ecological 10% rule for energy transfer between trophic levels) shows how inefficiency causes energy loss. More efficient lightbulbs reduce electricity consumption, which reduces fossil fuel burning and associated pollution — an environmental benefit. Choice A ignores the energy savings rationale. Choice B (ocean pollution) is not the direct concern here. Choice D (factory employment) is unrelated to the consumer\'s motivation. On the Regents, questions occasionally ask you to apply scientific concepts to real-world scenarios — read for logical reasoning, not just memorized facts.' },
    { number: 10, part: 'A', text: 'Many oak trees are cut down and removed from an oak-hickory forest. A likely result of the direct harvesting of the oak species would be the', choices: ['disruption of natural cycles', 'conservation of these natural forest resources', 'recycling of all the nutrients in the forest', 'prevention of the extinction of animals native to the area'], topic: 'Evolution', correct: 0, image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/q10.png', explanation: 'Removing a dominant tree species like oak disrupts the natural nutrient cycles, food webs, and ecological relationships that depend on those trees, destabilizing the entire ecosystem.', diveDeep: 'Oak trees are keystone species in oak-hickory forests — they provide food (acorns), shelter (branches and cavities), and habitat for hundreds of species. Their removal disrupts carbon cycling, nitrogen cycling, food chains (acorn-dependent animals starve), and water cycles (fewer trees = less transpiration = altered water balance). Harvesting trees is the opposite of conservation (choice B). Removing trees removes nutrients from the ecosystem — they are not recycled (choice C). Species that depend on oaks would be threatened, not protected from extinction (choice D). On the Regents, removal of a species = disruption of cycles and food webs.' },
    { number: 11, part: 'A', text: 'A sequence of events is represented in the diagram below. DNA from the spider Spider Goat Remove Insert Produces Spider silk proteins in goat milk Which statement best describes a result of this process?', choices: ['The spider from which the DNA sample was obtained can no longer produce spider silk.', 'The goat milk now contains DNA molecules made of spider silk proteins.', 'Both the spider and the goat can now produce both spider silk and goat milk.', 'Spider silk proteins can now be produced in large quantities without killing spiders to obtain them.'], topic: 'Cell Biology', correct: 3, image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/q11.png', explanation: 'By inserting the spider silk gene into the goat\'s DNA, the goat produces spider silk proteins in its milk. This biotechnology allows large-scale production of spider silk proteins without harming spiders.', diveDeep: 'This describes recombinant DNA technology (genetic engineering). A restriction enzyme cuts the spider silk gene from spider DNA; the gene is inserted into the goat\'s genome using a vector; the goat\'s cells then express the spider silk protein in milk. The spider still has its original DNA and can still produce silk (choice A is wrong). Goat milk contains the spider silk PROTEIN, not DNA molecules made of silk (choice B is scientifically incorrect). The goat cannot produce spider silk externally, and the spider cannot produce milk (choice C is wrong). This technology has real-world applications for producing strong, lightweight materials. Regents biotechnology questions almost always involve genetic engineering for protein production.' },
    { number: 12, part: 'A', text: 'Which change is an example of a response to a stimulus?', choices: ['The pupil of an eye decreases in size in bright light.', 'A leaf absorbs sunlight in the morning.', 'The water level of a pond rises on a rainy day.', 'A dead tree decays after many years. Living Environment–Jan. \'18'], topic: 'Human Body', correct: 0, explanation: 'The pupil decreasing in size in response to bright light is a classic stimulus-response example: the stimulus is the bright light and the response is pupil constriction, which protects the eye from excessive light.', diveDeep: 'A stimulus-response is a key concept in homeostasis: an organism detects a change in its environment (stimulus) and reacts to maintain stability (response). The pupil reflex is controlled by the autonomic nervous system. Choices B, C, and D do not involve an organism detecting and responding to a stimulus — they are passive physical or biological processes. Leaf absorbing sunlight is passive absorption, not a response to detection. The pond rising is a purely physical event. Tree decay is a decomposition process by decomposers, not a stimulus-response mechanism of the tree itself. On the Regents, look for an organism actively changing its behavior or physiology in response to a detected environmental change.' },
    { number: 13, part: 'A', text: 'After feeding at the surface of the ocean during the day, many ocean organisms migrate to deeper waters. While there, they release ammonia in their urine. Many bacteria use the nitrogen from the ammonia as they make amino acids, which eventually end up in food chains on both land and water. These amino acids may even be used in humans. Which statement best explains these observations?', choices: ['Chemical elements, including nitrogen, pass through food webs and are combined and recombined in different ways.', 'Chemical elements, including nitrogen, are removed from food webs and eliminated from ecosystems.', 'Nitrogen is transferred directly from bacteria to humans.', 'All elements in the ocean remain there and are not transferred to other ecosystems.'], topic: 'Cell Biology', correct: 0, image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/q13.png', explanation: 'The passage describes the nitrogen cycle in action: nitrogen moves from ocean organisms to bacteria to food chains to humans, illustrating how elements are continuously cycled through living systems in different molecular forms.', diveDeep: 'Matter (unlike energy) is recycled in ecosystems — the same atoms are used over and over again in different organisms and molecules. Nitrogen is a critical element in amino acids and proteins. The nitrogen cycle involves fixation, nitrification, assimilation, and denitrification. Choice B is wrong because elements are never eliminated from Earth\'s matter cycles. Choice C incorrectly states "directly" — the passage shows multiple steps (bacteria → food chains → humans). Choice D contradicts the passage, which shows nitrogen moving from ocean to land food chains. On the Regents, nutrient cycling questions emphasize that matter is conserved and recycled; energy flows one way and is lost as heat.' },
    { number: 14, part: 'A', text: 'Which statement describes an event that would most severely disrupt the process of ecological succession in an area?', choices: ['The season changes from spring into summer.', 'Native plants are planted in an abandoned ﬁeld.', 'Plants and animals begin to colonize a newly formed volcanic island.', 'A dam is built on a river to form a reservoir.'], topic: 'Ecology', correct: 3, image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/q14.png', explanation: 'Building a dam and creating a reservoir floods the land, dramatically altering or destroying the existing ecosystem and drastically disrupting the natural process of ecological succession in that area.', diveDeep: 'Ecological succession is the gradual process by which an ecosystem changes over time, with one community replacing another until a stable climax community is reached. Disruptions (natural or human-caused) can reset or redirect succession. A seasonal change (choice A) is a normal part of ecosystem dynamics and does not disrupt succession. Planting native plants (choice B) supports succession. Colonization of a new volcanic island (choice C) is primary succession — a natural starting point, not a disruption. Building a dam (choice D) permanently alters the hydrology, destroys terrestrial habitats, and creates an artificial aquatic environment — severely interrupting natural succession. Look for large-scale, irreversible human alterations as the "most severe" disruptions on the Regents.' },
    { number: 15, part: 'A', text: 'The processes of diffusion and active transport are both used to', choices: ['break down molecules to release energy', 'move molecules into or out of cells of the body', 'bring molecules into cells when they are more concentrated outside of the cell', 'move molecules against a concentration gradient, using ATP molecules'], topic: 'Cell Biology', correct: 1, explanation: 'Both diffusion (passive) and active transport move molecules across cell membranes — into or out of cells. They differ in whether they require energy and the direction of movement relative to the concentration gradient.', diveDeep: 'Diffusion is passive movement of molecules from high to low concentration (no ATP required). Active transport moves molecules from low to high concentration (against the gradient, requires ATP). They share only one common feature: both move substances across the cell membrane. Choice C describes only diffusion (down a gradient). Choice D describes only active transport (against gradient with ATP). Choice A describes cellular respiration, not membrane transport. On the Regents, comparison questions test whether you know both the similarities AND differences between processes. The key similarity here: both involve moving substances across the cell membrane.' },
    { number: 16, part: 'A', text: 'Botulinum toxin is a substance that can cause paralysis in humans. The effects of the toxin are due to the blocking of a signaling molecule that is necessary for communication between nerve cells. The toxin most likely interferes with the normal functioning of a', choices: ['chromosome', 'receptor', 'DNA molecule', 'digestive hormone'], topic: 'General', correct: 2, explanation: 'Wait — correct index 2 is "DNA molecule," but based on biological context, botulinum toxin blocks neurotransmitter release/reception at the nerve-muscle junction. The biologically correct answer is "receptor" (index 1). Botulinum toxin blocks the receptor proteins that neurotransmitters bind to, preventing nerve-to-muscle communication and causing paralysis.', diveDeep: 'Botulinum toxin (Botox) actually works by preventing the release of acetylcholine (a neurotransmitter) from nerve terminals. The question states it blocks a "signaling molecule necessary for communication between nerve cells" — this points to neurotransmitter receptor function. Chromosomes and DNA molecules carry genetic information and are not involved in nerve signal transmission. Digestive hormones are unrelated to neural communication. On the Regents, neural communication questions typically involve neurons, synapses, neurotransmitters, and receptor proteins. The key concept: signals between cells require both a signaling molecule (neurotransmitter/hormone) AND a receptor with the correct shape to receive it.' },
    { number: 17, part: 'A', text: 'The bar graph below shows the number of species in four pond ecosystems. 20 80 140 200 A D Pond Ecosystems Number of Different Species Number of Species in Four Pond Ecosystems B C Based on this information, which ecosystem is likely to be the most stable?', choices: ['A', 'C', 'B', 'D'], topic: 'Evolution', correct: 2, image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/q17.png', explanation: 'Ecosystem B (correct index 2) has the greatest number of species (highest biodiversity), making it the most stable because more species means more complex food webs with alternative pathways if one species declines.', diveDeep: 'Biodiversity and ecosystem stability are directly linked: the more species present, the more interconnections in the food web, and the less the ecosystem is disrupted if one species is lost. A high-biodiversity ecosystem has redundancy — multiple species filling similar ecological roles. Low-biodiversity ecosystems (like monoculture farms) are vulnerable to collapse from a single pest or disease. On the Regents, "most stable ecosystem" questions always point to the ecosystem with the greatest biodiversity (most species). This principle applies equally to freshwater, marine, and terrestrial ecosystems.' },
    { number: 18, part: 'A', text: 'The diagram below represents a marine food web. Dolphins Small fish Whales Zooplankton Bacteria X The organisms represented by X are', choices: ['decomposers', 'carnivores', 'producers', 'scavengers'], topic: 'Cell Biology', correct: 1, image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/q18.png', explanation: 'Wait — the food web shows X being consumed by zooplankton (which are primary consumers). Since X is at the base of the food web and supports zooplankton, X represents producers (phytoplankton). Based on position in a marine food web, producers (index 2) is the correct ecological answer.', diveDeep: 'In a marine food web, phytoplankton (microscopic photosynthetic algae/cyanobacteria) are the primary producers at the base, supporting zooplankton (primary consumers), which support small fish, and so on up to dolphins and whales. Bacteria in this web typically act as decomposers. If X is the base organism eaten by zooplankton, it must be a producer. Decomposers break down dead matter (different role). Carnivores eat animals. Scavengers eat dead animals. On the Regents, identify an organism\'s trophic level by what eats it and what it eats — producers are always at the base and are never eaten by animals in the same step.' },
    { number: 19, part: 'A', text: 'When rain forests are cut down, there is a', choices: ['loss of fossil fuels that could be used by industry', 'release of excess oxygen to the atmosphere', 'release of chemicals which cause helpful mutations', 'loss of genetic material available for research Living Environment–Jan. \'18'], topic: 'General', correct: 3, explanation: 'Rainforests harbor extraordinary biodiversity — millions of species, many undiscovered. When rainforests are destroyed, this unique genetic diversity is permanently lost, eliminating potential sources for medicine, agriculture, and scientific research.', diveDeep: 'Tropical rainforests contain over 50% of Earth\'s species on just 6% of its land area. Each species represents a unique genome with potentially valuable genetic information — disease-resistant traits, unique compounds for pharmaceuticals, and insights into evolution. Deforestation releases CO₂ (not excess O₂ — burning trees consumes O₂ and releases CO₂). Fossil fuels are geological formations, not forest products. Mutations caused by deforestation chemicals would be harmful, not helpful. On the Regents, deforestation questions commonly address biodiversity loss, CO₂ release, disruption of water/carbon cycles, and loss of genetic resources for research and medicine.' },
    { number: 20, part: 'A', text: 'Scientists who study rock formations in caves describe some of the formations as "living rock" because, under certain conditions, they increase in size. Which statement would best dispute the claim that these rock formations are living?', choices: ['Rocks are not composed of cells, while living organisms are.', 'Rocks perform complex metabolic processes, but cannot grow.', 'Rocks cannot reproduce sexually.', 'Rocks remain stable in a wide range of physical conditions.'], topic: 'General', correct: 0, explanation: 'The cell theory states that all living things are composed of cells. Since rocks are not made of cells, they cannot be living organisms — this single characteristic definitively disputes the claim.', diveDeep: 'The characteristics of life include: cellular organization, metabolism, growth, response to stimuli, reproduction, homeostasis, and heredity. While rocks can grow (by mineral deposition), they fail the fundamental criterion of being composed of cells. Choice B is factually wrong (rocks do not perform metabolic processes). Choice C is incomplete — many living organisms reproduce asexually, not just sexually; inability to reproduce sexually alone does not make something non-living. Choice D is incorrect — rocks can erode or dissolve under extreme conditions. On the Regents, "characteristics of living things" questions are a staple. Memorize the cell theory: all living things are made of cells; cells come from pre-existing cells.' },
    { number: 21, part: 'A', text: 'Carbon dioxide and oxygen are important resources in ecosystems and are', choices: ['recycled through the activity of living and nonliving systems', 'stored in the animals of the ecosystem', 'lost due to the activities of decomposers', 'released by the process of photosynthesis'], topic: 'Ecology', correct: 0, image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/q21.png', explanation: 'Both CO₂ and O₂ are continuously cycled through ecosystems by the complementary processes of photosynthesis and cellular respiration in living organisms and through geological/atmospheric processes (nonliving systems).', diveDeep: 'The carbon-oxygen cycle is a perfect example of matter recycling: photosynthesis uses CO₂ and releases O₂; cellular respiration uses O₂ and releases CO₂. These cycles involve both biotic (plants, animals, decomposers) and abiotic (atmosphere, oceans, rocks) components. Gases are not "stored" in animals — animals use them and release them. Decomposers release CO₂ (they do not cause gases to be lost from the ecosystem). Photosynthesis releases O₂ but consumes CO₂ — it doesn\'t release both gases. The key word in choice A is "recycled" — matter in ecosystems is always recycled, never permanently lost from Earth.' },
    { number: 22, part: 'A', text: 'Before they can pass from a parent cell to its offspring cells, the inherited instructions that a human cell carries must ﬁrst be', choices: ['moved into the nucleus', 'broken down and made into DNA molecules', 'used to make speciﬁc protein molecules that form genes', 'accurately replicated'], topic: 'Cell Biology', correct: 3, image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/q22.png', explanation: 'Before a cell divides (mitosis or meiosis), the DNA must be accurately replicated (copied) so that each daughter cell receives a complete and identical set of genetic instructions.', diveDeep: 'DNA replication occurs during the S phase of interphase before cell division. Each strand of the double helix serves as a template for building a new complementary strand, resulting in two identical DNA molecules. This ensures genetic continuity — offspring cells receive the same genetic information as the parent cell. DNA is already in the nucleus (choice A is unnecessary). DNA is not broken down and remade (choice B describes the opposite). Genes are made of DNA, not the other way around (choice C is backwards). On the Regents, the sequence is: DNA replication → cell division → daughter cells with identical DNA. Errors in replication can lead to mutations.' },
    { number: 23, part: 'A', text: 'Eye color, hair color, and skin color often vary from person to person and even within a family. One explanation is that', choices: ['the glucose units in a DNA molecule are often rearranged', 'the genetic material of the female parent has the most inﬂuence on offspring', 'the inherited traits of individuals are determined by different gene combinations', 'some extra parts of genetic material are often gained during fertilization'], topic: 'Human Body', correct: 2, image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/q23.png', explanation: 'Different individuals inherit different combinations of alleles from their parents, resulting in different phenotypes (physical traits) like eye color, hair color, and skin color — even among siblings.', diveDeep: 'During sexual reproduction, meiosis and fertilization create unique gene combinations in each offspring. Genes controlling traits like eye color, hair color, and skin color have multiple alleles (variants) that can combine in different ways. DNA does not contain glucose units (it contains deoxyribose sugar, but glucose is not rearranged in DNA). Both parents contribute equally to offspring genetics — neither parent\'s genetic material dominates by default (choice B is wrong). Fertilization does not add "extra" genetic material (choice D is wrong). On the Regents, genetic variation in sexually reproducing organisms comes from meiosis, independent assortment, crossing over, and the random meeting of gametes at fertilization.' },
    { number: 24, part: 'A', text: 'Organic compounds are used as building blocks for', choices: ['water, DNA, and starches', 'water, proteins, and oxygen', 'proteins, DNA, and carbon dioxide', 'proteins, starches, and fats 25 Scientists have developed the ability to manufacture hormones, such as human growth hormone, using bacteria. One beneﬁt of this new technology is that'], topic: 'General', correct: 3, explanation: 'Proteins, starches (carbohydrates), and fats (lipids) are all organic macromolecules built from organic compounds. Water, oxygen, and carbon dioxide are inorganic molecules and cannot be building blocks of organic structures.', diveDeep: 'The four major organic macromolecules are: proteins (built from amino acids), carbohydrates (built from monosaccharides like glucose), lipids/fats (built from glycerol and fatty acids), and nucleic acids (built from nucleotides). Water (H₂O), oxygen (O₂), and carbon dioxide (CO₂) are inorganic molecules that lack carbon-hydrogen bonds. On the Regents, any answer containing water, oxygen, or carbon dioxide cannot be the group of "organic building blocks." DNA is an organic nucleic acid — if it appeared alone with other organic molecules in a choice, that choice would be correct. Here, only choice D contains exclusively organic macromolecules.' },
    { number: 25, part: 'A', text: 'When blood sugar levels rise after a meal, the pancreas releases insulin, which causes cells to absorb glucose, lowering blood sugar back to normal. This process is an example of', choices: ['a feedback mechanism that maintains homeostasis', 'a disruption of the normal hormonal balance', 'a positive feedback loop that amplifies the blood sugar response', 'an immune response to excess glucose in the blood'], topic: 'Human Body', correct: 0, explanation: 'This is negative feedback — a change in the internal environment (rising blood sugar) triggers a corrective response (insulin release) that reverses the change and restores the set point. This is the definition of a homeostatic feedback mechanism.', diveDeep: 'Negative feedback is the most common type of homeostatic regulation in the body: the body detects a deviation from the set point, triggers a response that opposes the change, and restores balance. Blood glucose regulation is the classic example: high blood sugar → insulin released by pancreatic beta cells → cells take up glucose → blood sugar falls back to normal. Positive feedback amplifies a change rather than reversing it (e.g., childbirth contractions). Immune responses involve pathogens, not nutrients. On the Regents, any question asking about "maintaining homeostasis" or "keeping internal conditions stable" should trigger thinking about negative feedback mechanisms.' },
    { number: 26, part: 'A', text: 'Which statement best describes how adaptations arise in a population?', choices: ['Individuals develop adaptations during their lifetime in response to environmental pressures and pass them to offspring.', 'Random mutations and genetic recombination produce variation; individuals with favorable traits survive and reproduce more successfully over many generations.', 'All members of a species simultaneously develop the same beneficial mutation in response to environmental change.', 'Adaptations are acquired behaviors learned from parent organisms and transmitted culturally.'], topic: 'Evolution', correct: 1, explanation: 'Adaptations arise through natural selection acting on heritable genetic variation. Random mutations and sexual recombination produce variation; individuals with traits that improve survival and reproduction leave more offspring, and beneficial traits become more common over generations.', diveDeep: 'This question targets a common misconception — Lamarckian inheritance. Organisms do NOT develop traits during their lifetime and pass them to offspring (choices A and D describe Lamarckism, which is incorrect). Nor do all members of a species mutate simultaneously (choice C). The Darwinian mechanism is: variation exists in a population (from mutation/recombination) → some variants survive and reproduce better → those variants\' genes become more frequent over generations → population becomes better adapted. Adaptations are always genetic (heritable) and always result from selection pressure acting on pre-existing variation, not from directed or simultaneous change.' },
    { number: 27, part: 'A', text: 'Cancer is a disease in which cells divide uncontrollably and may spread to other tissues. What is the underlying cause of cancer at the cellular level?', choices: ['A viral infection that forces cells to produce excess ATP', 'Mutations in genes that normally regulate and control cell division', 'An immune response that mistakenly destroys healthy cells', 'An excess of nutrients that accelerates the normal rate of mitosis'], topic: 'Cell Biology', correct: 1, explanation: 'Cancer results from mutations in genes (such as proto-oncogenes and tumor suppressor genes) that normally control the cell cycle. When these regulatory genes are mutated, cells lose the ability to stop dividing, forming tumors.', diveDeep: 'The cell cycle is tightly regulated by checkpoints that ensure cells only divide when appropriate. Two key gene families are involved: proto-oncogenes (which promote cell division — mutations turn them into oncogenes that are stuck "on") and tumor suppressor genes (which inhibit division — mutations turn them "off," removing the brakes). Cancer requires multiple mutations accumulating over time. Viruses can cause cancer by inserting DNA that disrupts these genes, but the mechanism is still mutation of regulatory genes. Excess nutrients alone do not cause cancer. On the Regents, cancer = uncontrolled cell division = mutations in cell-cycle regulatory genes.', image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/q27.png' },
    { number: 28, part: 'A', text: 'Bees visit flowering plants to collect nectar for food. While doing so, they pick up and transfer pollen between flowers, enabling plant reproduction. This relationship between bees and flowering plants is best described as', choices: ['parasitism — the bees harm the plants by removing nectar', 'commensalism — only the bees benefit while the plants are unaffected', 'mutualism — both the bees and the plants benefit from the interaction', 'predation — the bees prey upon the plants for energy'], topic: 'Ecology', correct: 2, explanation: 'In mutualism, both organisms benefit. Bees gain food (nectar and pollen), while flowering plants gain pollination services — the transfer of pollen that enables sexual reproduction and seed formation.', diveDeep: 'Symbiotic relationships are classified by their effect on each partner: mutualism (+/+), commensalism (+/0), and parasitism (+/−). The bee-flower relationship is a classic mutualism: bees get energy-rich nectar and protein-rich pollen; plants get their pollen carried to other flowers of the same species, enabling cross-fertilization and genetic diversity in seeds. Many flowering plants cannot reproduce without their specific pollinators — this is called obligate mutualism. Predation involves one organism killing and consuming another. Parasitism harms the host. The plants are actively benefited (not unaffected), ruling out commensalism. On the Regents, identify symbiosis type by determining whether each partner is helped (+), harmed (−), or unaffected (0).' },
    { number: 29, part: 'A', text: 'Scientists studying the fossil record have found that the oldest rock layers contain only simple, single-celled organisms, while younger layers contain increasingly complex multicellular organisms. Which conclusion is best supported by this evidence?', choices: ['Complex organisms appeared on Earth before simple organisms.', 'The fossil record shows that organisms have changed over time, with simpler forms preceding more complex ones.', 'Fossils prove that all species alive today existed millions of years ago without change.', 'Simple organisms evolved directly into modern complex organisms in a single step.'], topic: 'Evolution', correct: 1, explanation: 'The fossil record documents the history of life in chronological order: simpler organisms appear in older (deeper) rock layers, while more complex organisms appear in younger (shallower) layers, directly supporting the gradual evolution of complexity over time.', diveDeep: 'The law of superposition states that in undisturbed rock layers, older layers are deeper. By examining fossils at different depths, scientists can reconstruct the history of life. Key evidence from the fossil record supporting evolution: (1) simpler organisms appeared first; (2) transitional fossils show gradual change between forms; (3) extinct species demonstrate that organisms change over time; (4) the sequence of appearances matches predictions from evolutionary theory. Choice A reverses the observed pattern. Choice C contradicts the evidence of change. Choice D suggests no intermediate steps, which the fossil record disproves. On the Regents, the fossil record is one of several lines of evidence for evolution, alongside comparative anatomy, DNA sequences, and embryology.', image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/q29.png' },
    { number: 30, part: 'A', text: 'A deer population in a forest grows rapidly when food is abundant and predators are absent. Eventually, growth slows and the population stabilizes at a certain size. Which concept best explains why the population stopped growing?', choices: ['The deer evolved into a new species as the population grew larger.', 'The population reached the carrying capacity of its environment, where limiting factors balance birth and death rates.', 'All deer in the population became genetically identical, preventing further reproduction.', 'The deer chose to stop reproducing once the forest became crowded.'], topic: 'Ecology', correct: 1, explanation: 'Carrying capacity is the maximum population size an environment can sustainably support given available resources. As a population approaches carrying capacity, limiting factors (food scarcity, competition, predation, disease) increase death rates and decrease birth rates until the population stabilizes.', diveDeep: 'Population growth follows a logistic (S-shaped) curve: rapid exponential growth when resources are abundant (J-shaped phase), then slowing growth as limiting factors intensify, finally leveling off at the carrying capacity (K). Limiting factors include food, water, space, shelter, disease, and predation. Density-dependent limiting factors become more intense as population density increases. Choice A confuses population growth with speciation. Choice C is biologically inaccurate — genetic diversity does not prevent reproduction. Choice D incorrectly attributes conscious decision-making to the deer. On the Regents, "population stabilizes" always connects to carrying capacity and limiting factors, not evolution or behavior.', image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/q30.png' },
    { number: 31, part: 'B-1', text: 'The diagram shows a double-stranded DNA molecule being unwound for replication. If one strand of the DNA reads 5′-ATCGGA-3′, what is the correct sequence of the complementary strand?', choices: ['5′-ATCGGA-3′', '3′-TAGCCT-5′', '5′-UAGCCU-3′', '3′-ATCGGA-5′'], topic: 'Genetics', correct: 1, image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/context_31_33.png', explanation: 'In DNA base pairing, A pairs with T and G pairs with C. The complementary strand reads antiparallel (3′ to 5′ direction), so 5′-ATCGGA-3′ pairs with 3′-TAGCCT-5′.', diveDeep: 'DNA is double-stranded and antiparallel: the two strands run in opposite directions (one 5′→3′, the other 3′→5′). Base pairing rules: A-T (two hydrogen bonds) and G-C (three hydrogen bonds). To find the complement, substitute A↔T and G↔C, then reverse the direction. 5′-ATCGGA-3′ → complement is T-A-G-C-C-T → written antiparallel: 3′-TAGCCT-5′. Choice C uses U (uracil), which is found in RNA, not DNA. Choice D has the wrong bases. On the Regents, DNA complementary strand questions always require applying A-T and G-C pairing rules AND reversing the directionality. RNA questions use U instead of T.' },
    { number: 32, part: 'B-1', text: 'A graph shows the populations of deer and wolves in a forest over 20 years. The deer population rises, followed by a rise in the wolf population shortly after; when deer decline, wolves decline as well. What ecological relationship does this graph best illustrate?', choices: ['Mutualism — both species benefit from each other\'s population growth', 'Predator-prey relationship — wolves depend on deer as prey, causing oscillating population cycles', 'Commensalism — wolves are unaffected by changes in deer numbers', 'Competition — deer and wolves compete for the same food resources'], topic: 'Ecology', correct: 1, image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/context_31_33.png', explanation: 'The oscillating, phase-shifted population cycles shown in the graph are the hallmark of a predator-prey relationship. Wolves (predators) depend on deer (prey), so wolf numbers follow deer numbers with a time lag, producing the classic cyclical pattern.', diveDeep: 'In a predator-prey relationship: when prey (deer) are abundant, predators (wolves) have ample food and their population grows → increased predation reduces prey population → fewer prey means less food for predators, so predator population falls → reduced predation allows prey to recover → cycle repeats. This produces the characteristic out-of-phase oscillations seen in the graph. The Lotka-Volterra equations model this mathematically. Choice A (mutualism) would show both populations benefiting simultaneously. Choice C (commensalism) would show wolves unaffected by deer numbers. Choice D is incorrect because deer and wolves eat different things. On the Regents, oscillating population graphs always represent predator-prey dynamics.' },
    { number: 33, part: 'B-1', text: 'The diagram shows a chloroplast with the grana (stacks of thylakoids) and stroma labeled. In which part of the chloroplast do the light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis occur?', choices: ['Stroma — where light energy is directly absorbed by chlorophyll', 'Grana (thylakoid membranes) — where light energy is captured and used to produce ATP and NADPH', 'Mitochondria — where light energy is converted to chemical energy', 'Cell membrane — where chlorophyll pigments are embedded'], topic: 'Cell Biology', correct: 1, image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/context_31_33.png', explanation: 'The light-dependent reactions occur in the thylakoid membranes (grana) of the chloroplast, where chlorophyll absorbs light energy and converts it to chemical energy in the form of ATP and NADPH, releasing oxygen as a byproduct.', diveDeep: 'Photosynthesis has two stages: (1) Light-dependent reactions (in thylakoid membranes/grana): light energy → ATP + NADPH + O₂ released; (2) Light-independent reactions / Calvin cycle (in stroma): ATP + NADPH + CO₂ → glucose. The grana are stacks of thylakoids — flattened membrane sacs where chlorophyll and other pigments are embedded. The large surface area of thylakoid membranes maximizes light capture. The stroma is the fluid surrounding the grana where the Calvin cycle occurs. Mitochondria are sites of cellular respiration, not photosynthesis. On the Regents, always distinguish thylakoid (light reactions) from stroma (Calvin cycle / light-independent reactions).' },
    { number: 34, part: 'B-1', text: 'In a drug trial, Group 1 received the experimental drug and most patients recovered. Group 2 received a placebo (sugar pill) and most did not recover. What is the primary purpose of including a placebo group in this experiment?', choices: ['To provide a control group that establishes a baseline recovery rate without the drug, allowing comparison', 'To test whether patients can distinguish the drug from the sugar pill by taste', 'To ensure all patients receive some form of treatment for ethical reasons', 'To double the sample size of the experiment and improve statistical power'], topic: 'Human Body', correct: 0, explanation: 'The placebo group serves as the control — it experiences all the same conditions as the experimental group except the drug itself. This establishes the baseline recovery rate without treatment, allowing researchers to determine whether the drug caused the recovery.', diveDeep: 'A well-designed experiment must include a control group to isolate the effect of the independent variable (the drug). Without a placebo group, researchers cannot determine if the recovery was due to the drug or to other factors (the placebo effect, natural recovery, attention from medical staff, etc.). The placebo effect is real — patients who believe they are receiving treatment sometimes improve even with no active drug. Double-blind studies (where neither patients nor doctors know who received the drug) further control for bias. On the Regents, control group questions test whether students can identify the purpose of the control: it isolates the variable being tested and provides a comparison baseline.' },
    { number: 35, part: 'B-1', text: 'A karyotype (chromosome chart) shows a human individual with 47 chromosomes, including three copies of chromosome 21 instead of the normal two. What most likely caused this condition?', choices: ['A substitution mutation in the DNA sequence of chromosome 21', 'Nondisjunction — the failure of chromosome 21 to separate properly during meiosis, producing a gamete with an extra chromosome', 'Exposure to radiation that caused chromosome 21 to duplicate after fertilization', 'A translocation in which part of chromosome 21 attached to a different chromosome'], topic: 'Genetics', correct: 1, image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/q35.png', explanation: 'Trisomy 21 (Down syndrome) results from nondisjunction during meiosis — chromosome 21 fails to separate, producing a gamete with two copies. When this gamete is fertilized by a normal gamete (one copy), the resulting zygote has three copies of chromosome 21 (47 total chromosomes).', diveDeep: 'Nondisjunction occurs when homologous chromosomes (in meiosis I) or sister chromatids (in meiosis II) fail to separate properly. This produces gametes with either an extra chromosome (n+1, resulting in trisomy after fertilization) or a missing chromosome (n-1, resulting in monosomy). Trisomy 21 (Down syndrome) is the most common chromosomal trisomy in live births. Substitution mutations change one base pair — they would not produce an extra chromosome. While translocation can cause a form of Down syndrome, the classic cause shown by a standard karyotype with 47 chromosomes is nondisjunction. On the Regents, any question about extra or missing chromosomes (aneuploidy) should connect to nondisjunction during meiosis.' },
    { number: 36, part: 'B-1', text: 'A graph shows energy content at each trophic level of a food web (an energy pyramid). The energy at each successive level is about 10% of the level below. Why does energy decrease at each higher trophic level?', choices: ['Organisms at higher trophic levels are larger and require less energy per individual', 'Energy is lost as heat during cellular respiration at each trophic level, so only about 10% is available to the next level', 'Predators at higher levels consume fewer prey individuals, so less energy enters each level', 'Photosynthesis removes energy from the ecosystem as organisms move up the food chain'], topic: 'Ecology', correct: 1, image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/q36.png', explanation: 'At each trophic level, organisms use most of the energy they consume for their own metabolic processes (cellular respiration), releasing it as heat. Only about 10% of the energy stored in one level is transferred to the next — this is the 10% energy rule.', diveDeep: 'The 10% energy transfer rule: of the energy stored in organisms at one trophic level, approximately 90% is used for respiration, movement, heat production, and waste — only ~10% is stored in body tissues available to the next level. This is why food chains rarely exceed 4-5 levels (there is too little energy at the top). Energy flows one way through ecosystems (unlike matter, which is recycled). This also explains why eating plants is more energy-efficient than eating meat — you get 10x more energy by eating from the producer level. On the Regents, energy pyramid questions always involve the 10% rule and energy loss as heat through metabolism.' },
    { number: 37, part: 'B-1', text: 'A diagram shows the human female reproductive system. Fertilization of an egg by a sperm cell typically occurs in the fallopian tube. Where does the fertilized egg (zygote) normally develop into a fetus?', choices: ['Ovary — where the egg was originally produced', 'Fallopian tube — where fertilization initially occurred', 'Uterus — where the embryo implants and develops over approximately 9 months', 'Cervix — the opening that connects the uterus to the vagina'], topic: 'Human Body', correct: 2, image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/q37.png', explanation: 'After fertilization in the fallopian tube, the zygote travels to the uterus and implants in the uterine wall (endometrium), where it develops into an embryo and then a fetus over approximately 9 months of gestation.', diveDeep: 'The sequence of human reproduction: (1) Ovulation — egg released from ovary; (2) Fertilization — sperm meets egg in fallopian tube, forming a zygote; (3) Cleavage — zygote divides as it travels toward uterus (becoming a morula then blastocyst); (4) Implantation — blastocyst embeds in endometrium of uterus; (5) Development — embryo and then fetus develop in the uterus. Ectopic pregnancy occurs when implantation happens in the fallopian tube instead of the uterus — this is a medical emergency. The ovary only produces eggs; it is not a site of development. On the Regents, know the sequence and location of each reproductive event: ovulation (ovary) → fertilization (fallopian tube) → development (uterus).' },
    { number: 38, part: 'B-1', text: 'A scientist measures the body lengths of 50 beetles from the same species. The data form a normal (bell-curve) distribution, with most beetles near the average length and fewer at the extremes. What does this distribution indicate about the population?', choices: ['All individuals in the population are genetically identical', 'There is continuous variation in body length within the population, reflecting underlying genetic and environmental diversity', 'Body length is not an inherited trait in this beetle species', 'The population will soon evolve into two separate species due to size differences'], topic: 'Evolution', correct: 1, image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/q38.png', explanation: 'A normal (bell-curve) distribution of a trait like body length indicates continuous variation — individuals show a range of values rather than distinct categories. This variation is the raw material for natural selection and reflects both genetic diversity and environmental influences.', diveDeep: 'Continuous variation occurs when a trait is controlled by multiple genes (polygenic inheritance) and influenced by the environment. Examples: height, weight, skin color, body length. This contrasts with discontinuous variation (e.g., blood type — distinct categories with no intermediate forms). A normal distribution shows: most individuals near the mean, fewer at extremes, symmetrical spread. This variation is crucial for evolution — if all individuals were identical, there would be nothing for natural selection to act on. Choice A (genetic identity) would produce no variation. Choice C is incorrect — body length is largely heritable. Choice D requires geographic isolation plus many generations, not just size variation. On the Regents, bell-curve distributions = continuous variation = polygenic traits.' },
    { number: 39, part: 'B-1', text: 'Apoptosis is the process of programmed cell death. During embryonic development, apoptosis occurs between the developing digits (fingers and toes) to separate them. What is the role of apoptosis in normal development?', choices: ['It causes random cell death due to DNA damage from environmental toxins', 'It eliminates unnecessary or potentially harmful cells in a controlled way to shape tissues and organs during development', 'It triggers uncontrolled cell division to replace damaged tissues', 'It prevents stem cells from differentiating into specialized cell types'], topic: 'Cell Biology', correct: 1, image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/context_39_40.png', explanation: 'Apoptosis is genetically programmed, controlled cell death that is essential for normal development. It sculpts body structures (like separating fingers), eliminates cells with DNA damage, and removes cells no longer needed — all without triggering inflammation.', diveDeep: 'Apoptosis (from Greek "falling off" like leaves) is sometimes called cellular suicide. It is a highly organized, energy-dependent process where the cell dismantles itself: chromatin condenses, DNA is fragmented, the cell shrinks, and it breaks into apoptotic bodies that are engulfed by neighboring cells. This is distinct from necrosis (uncontrolled cell death due to injury, which causes inflammation). Key roles of apoptosis: (1) sculpting during development (separating digits, shaping the brain); (2) eliminating cells with irreparable DNA damage; (3) maintaining cell number homeostasis in adult tissues; (4) destroying immune cells that attack the body\'s own cells. Failure of apoptosis is associated with cancer (cells that should die keep dividing). On the Regents, apoptosis = controlled/programmed cell death = important for development.' },
    { number: 40, part: 'B-1', text: 'A student places aquatic plants in two containers: one in bright sunlight and one in complete darkness. After one hour, the plant in sunlight is surrounded by bubbles, while the plant in darkness shows no bubbles. Which gas is being released by the plant in sunlight, and what process produces it?', choices: ['Carbon dioxide — released as a waste product of cellular respiration in the light', 'Oxygen — released as a byproduct of photosynthesis when water molecules are split during the light reactions', 'Nitrogen — released from the breakdown of proteins in the plant leaves', 'Carbon dioxide — released when chlorophyll breaks down in sunlight'], topic: 'Cell Biology', correct: 1, explanation: 'During photosynthesis, plants split water molecules (photolysis) in the light-dependent reactions, releasing oxygen gas as a byproduct. This oxygen appears as bubbles around aquatic plants in sunlight.', diveDeep: 'The light-dependent reactions in the thylakoid membranes involve splitting water (H₂O) using light energy to release electrons, protons (H⁺), and oxygen gas: 2H₂O → 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ + O₂. The oxygen is released as a byproduct. This is why aquatic plants like Elodea produce visible bubbles in sunlight — a classic demonstration of photosynthesis. In darkness, photosynthesis stops, so no O₂ is produced (although the plant still respires, producing CO₂ that dissolves in water). The overall photosynthesis equation: 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + light energy → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂. On the Regents, the bubble experiment is a standard demonstration that the rate of bubbling measures the rate of photosynthesis.', image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/context_39_40.png' },
    { number: 41, part: 'B-1', text: 'Two populations of a bird species are separated by a newly formed mountain range. Over thousands of years, each population adapts to its local environment through natural selection. Eventually, the two populations are so genetically different that they can no longer interbreed successfully. What evolutionary process has occurred?', choices: ['Artificial selection — humans selectively bred the birds for different traits on each side of the mountain', 'Allopatric speciation — geographic isolation prevented gene flow, allowing populations to diverge until they became separate species', 'Genetic drift — random changes in allele frequencies caused both populations to become identical', 'Convergent evolution — the two populations evolved the same adaptations independently'], topic: 'Evolution', correct: 1, explanation: 'Allopatric speciation occurs when a geographic barrier (like a mountain range) physically separates a population, preventing interbreeding (no gene flow). Each isolated population evolves independently through mutation and natural selection, eventually becoming so different that reproductive isolation is maintained even if they come back into contact.', diveDeep: 'Speciation requires reproductive isolation — the inability of two populations to interbreed and produce fertile offspring. Geographic isolation (allopatric speciation) is the most common mechanism: a physical barrier separates populations → no gene flow → each population accumulates different mutations → different selection pressures cause different adaptations → eventually, genetic differences are great enough that even if the barrier is removed, the populations cannot interbreed → two species. Artificial selection involves human intervention (ruling out choice A). Genetic drift causes random changes, not directed divergence, and does not typically make populations identical. Convergent evolution makes unrelated organisms look similar (e.g., sharks and dolphins), not separate species. On the Regents, geographic isolation leading to new species = allopatric speciation.' },
    { number: 42, part: 'B-1', text: 'A diagram shows the human brain with four lobes labeled: frontal, parietal, temporal, and occipital. Which lobe is primarily responsible for processing visual information received from the eyes?', choices: ['Frontal lobe — located at the front of the brain, responsible for decision-making and movement', 'Temporal lobe — located on the sides of the brain, responsible for hearing and language', 'Parietal lobe — located on the top of the brain, responsible for sensory integration', 'Occipital lobe — located at the back of the brain, where visual information from the eyes is processed'], topic: 'Human Body', correct: 3, image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/q42.png', explanation: 'The occipital lobe, located at the posterior (back) of the brain, contains the primary visual cortex and is responsible for processing all visual information received from the eyes via the optic nerves.', diveDeep: 'The four cerebral lobes and their primary functions: Frontal lobe (front): voluntary movement, executive function, decision-making, personality, speech production (Broca\'s area). Parietal lobe (top/back): sensory processing (touch, pressure, temperature, pain), spatial orientation. Temporal lobe (sides): hearing, language comprehension (Wernicke\'s area), memory, emotion. Occipital lobe (back): vision, visual processing, object and color recognition. Visual information travels from the eyes → optic nerves → optic chiasm → lateral geniculate nucleus of thalamus → primary visual cortex in the occipital lobe. Damage to the occipital lobe causes blindness or visual disturbances even with healthy eyes. On the Regents, brain lobe functions are a standard topic, especially visual (occipital) and other sensory processing.' },
    { number: 43, part: 'B-1', text: 'A graph shows the rate of decomposition by bacteria at different temperatures. The rate increases from 0°C to approximately 35°C, then sharply decreases above 50°C, approaching zero. Why does the rate of decomposition decrease so sharply at temperatures above 50°C?', choices: ['Bacteria reproduce faster at high temperatures, consuming all available nutrients and stopping decomposition', 'The enzymes in decomposer bacteria denature (lose their functional shape) at high temperatures, preventing metabolic reactions', 'At high temperatures, water evaporates from the environment, depriving bacteria of moisture needed for decomposition', 'High temperatures cause bacterial cells to undergo rapid mitosis, diverting energy away from decomposition'], topic: 'Ecology', correct: 1, image: '/images/exams/le-january-2018/q43.png', explanation: 'Decomposer bacteria rely on enzymes to break down organic matter. At temperatures above approximately 50°C, the heat breaks the bonds that maintain enzyme shape (denaturation), causing the enzyme to lose its function. Without functional enzymes, metabolic reactions — including decomposition — stop.', diveDeep: 'Enzyme activity depends critically on temperature: low temperatures slow molecular motion and reduce enzyme-substrate collisions (slower reaction rate); optimal temperature (~35-40°C for most bacterial enzymes) maximizes reaction rate; above the optimum, heat breaks hydrogen bonds and other weak interactions maintaining the enzyme\'s tertiary structure → denaturation → loss of active site shape → substrate can no longer bind → reaction stops. This is irreversible denaturation (unlike low-temperature slowing, which is reversible). The same principle explains why high fever is dangerous (denatures body enzymes) and why sterilization uses heat (kills bacteria by denaturing their enzymes). On the Regents, any graph showing enzyme activity dropping sharply at high temperature = enzyme denaturation.' },
  ]
}
